<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Adaptive launcher icon. minSdk is 30, so this v26-qualified resource always
  matches and no legacy square fallback is needed.

  The foreground and monochrome layers are GENERATED, by
  scripts/build-brand-icons.mjs, from the same geometry definition that renders
  the phone's icon — so the companion app looks like the app it companions by
  construction rather than by someone remembering to re-copy it. That is the
  change Story 21.4 made: they used to be a hand-made downscale of
  assets/adaptive-icon.png, which is how they came to be a whole rebrand out of
  date and still drawing the pre-rebrand blue wallet. `yarn icons:check` now re-renders
  all four densities of both layers and compares bytes, on pre-push and in
  ci-quality-gates.yml.

  The <monochrome> layer is what Android 13+ tints for a themed icon. It is a
  separate asset rather than a second reference to @mipmap/ic_launcher_foreground
  because AdaptiveIconDrawable.getMonochrome() only promises that callers CAN
  tint it — and a launcher that draws it untinted would put the beam's yellow
  on a themed field, which is two colours and therefore not a monochrome icon.
  Below API 33 the element is inert: Android 11's AdaptiveIconDrawable tests the
  child tag against "background" and "foreground" and continues past anything
  else, so it costs the Wear OS 3 devices minSdk 30 admits exactly nothing.

  It appears unread ABOVE API 33 too, on Wear specifically, and that is measured
  rather than assumed: the Wear OS 5 stock launcher (ClockworkSysUiGoogle.apk,
  API 34) references getForeground, getBackground and AdaptiveIconDrawable but
  has no reference to getMonochrome at all, and framework method names are the
  one thing R8 cannot rename. The limits of that scan are written down in
  docs/design/wear-launcher-verification/. So this layer is kept for the lint
  check it satisfies, for parity with what the phone declares, and because it
  costs ~7 KB across four densities — NOT on a belief that Wear tints it.
-->
<adaptive-icon xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android">
    <background android:drawable="@color/ic_launcher_background" />
    <foreground android:drawable="@mipmap/ic_launcher_foreground" />
    <monochrome android:drawable="@mipmap/ic_launcher_monochrome" />
</adaptive-icon>
